<section id="documents">
  <h3>Automating 15,000 Risk Registers</h3>
  <p class="case-lede">UNDP runs projects in 170 countries. Each carries a project document—often a few dozen pages, sometimes hundreds—and each needs a risk register: the risks, their causes, their treatment, scored impact × likelihood. The register was written by reading the document from end to end.</p>

  <div class="pair">
    <div>
      <h3>Problem</h3>
      <p>The reading was slow and repetitive, and almost identical in shape from one project to the next. The output was eight fields in a table.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Build</h3>
      <p>It splits a project document into blocks, extracts a structured record from each, and assembles the register.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure class="plate">
    <div class="record" role="group" aria-label="One extracted record">
      <div class="record-head"><span>Output · one record</span><span>Iraq · 2006 project</span></div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Event</dt><dd>Deterioration of the security situation</dd>
        <dt>Cause</dt><dd>Political instability, civil unrest, or armed conflict in the region</dd>
        <dt>Impacts</dt><dd>Delays or complete halt; harm to staff and communities; damage to infrastructure; failure to deliver water and sanitation access.</dd>
        <dt>Treatment</dt><dd>Comprehensive security plan, staff training, coordination with local authorities, contingency plans for evacuation or suspension…</dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="record-scores"><span>Impact <b>4</b></span><span>Likelihood <b>3</b></span><span>Significance <b>12</b></span></div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Verbatim from the model’s output, trimmed for space. <a href="https://emanuele.co/automating-risk-registers-at-undp-v3/">Full text in the working notes</a>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>What testing changed</h3>
  <div class="bench" role="group" aria-label="Processing time on a 21-page document">
    <div class="bench-row"><span class="bench-label">Sequential</span><span class="bench-bar"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><b>531s</b></div>
    <div class="bench-row"><span class="bench-label">Parallel</span><span class="bench-bar"><i style="width:11.3%"></i></span><b>60s</b></div>
    <p class="bench-note">Same document, same model, same analysis. Parallel API calls across 8 cores: about nine times faster.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="changes">
    <li><b>Run the calls at once.</b> The analysis was per-chunk and independent. The output was identical; only the waiting was unnecessary.</li>
    <li><b>Say when there is nothing.</b> Chunks with no risks were inventing entries. One line—“reply NO RISK DETECTED if none”—removed them.</li>
    <li><b>Align with the policy.</b> I rewrote the prompt against UNDP’s ERM policy: the four treatment types, and a worked example matching the output I wanted.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>I checked quality by running the same document through the old and new prompts and comparing field by field. Both found the same top risk, at impact 4 and likelihood 3; the new prompt named the project’s own objectives in the impacts. That work became my MBA dissertation: iterative one-shot learning for document evaluation.</p>

  <h3>Adoption</h3>
  <p>Analysts upload a project document and download the register as a workbook—no scripts, no setup, nothing to install. 15,000+ documents have gone through it. The reviewer starts from a draft instead of a blank page, and the entries arrive already in UNDP’s categories and 1–5 scales, so they drop straight into the existing register.</p>
  <p class="links"><a href="https://emanuele.co/automating-risk-registers-at-undp-v3/">Working notes</a> <a href="https://emanuele.co/automating-risk-registers-at-undp-v2/">The interface</a> <a href="https://emanuele.co/increase-llm-speed/">The parallelisation test</a></p>
</section>

<section id="performance">
  <h3>The Performance App: The Mother of All Dashboards</h3>
  <p class="case-lede">The Performance App is UNDP’s data dashboard. Finance, workforce, audit and impact data used to sit in separate systems and dashboards; now senior management can see them in one place. I built it single-handedly: the architecture, the SQL models, and the Python ETL behind it, including the warehouse, an OLAP cube, and Salesforce and operational extracts. It saves the organization over $300,000 a year in data maintenance costs.</p>

  <figure class="plate">
    <img src="/media/work/undp-overview-detail.png" alt="The UNDP Performance App: corporate report card with five strategic areas and indicator scores" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>The live Performance App: 22 indicators across five areas, with global, regional and country-office views. <a href="https://performance.undp.org">performance.undp.org</a></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>It scores organisational units on 22 indicators across five areas, and expanded beyond dashboards into operational workflows.</p>

  <h3>What it changed</h3>
  <div class="sesp">
    <div><span>From</span><b>15<i>%</i></b></div>
    <div class="sesp-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
    <div><span>To</span><b>75<i>%</i></b></div>
    <p>Environmental and social safeguards compliance, in three months. My work on the app helped: it made targets visible and drill-downs possible. Country offices did the compliance work.</p>
  </div>
  <p>SESP is UNDP’s environmental and social safeguards standard. Compliance is the share of projects meeting it, as tracked in the Performance App. Making the target visible—and giving every office the same view of it—is what moved it.</p>
</section>
</section>

<section class="case" id="reasoning">
  <h2>Building a machine that teaches itself</h2>
  <p class="case-lede">I’m building a universal reasoning engine: a machine that learns, reasons and improves its own methods. It is intended to invent the last thing humanity will ever need to invent.</p>
  <p>Today’s AI is trained once, at enormous cost, and its knowledge is locked inside billions of numbers. The human brain runs on about 20 watts and learns from a handful of examples. That tells me the current way of building AI is a choice, not a law of nature.</p>
  <p>So I’m separating intelligence from knowledge. A small, simple engine reasons over a growing graph of facts, relationships and reusable ways of thinking. It adds what it learns, corrects what it gets wrong and saves a better method when it finds one. Other machines can share that knowledge without retraining.</p>
  <p>If it works, intelligence becomes cumulative, inspectable and cheap enough to run on your own computer.</p>
  <p><strong>It could reach frontier-level capability without frontier-scale training.</strong></p>
</section>

